jkastner@netcom.com (Jason Kastner) writes:


>I've got version 3.1.1 and have got a few questions. Hopefully some
>more experienced people can set me straight.

>Possible Spoliers ahead (but minor ones if any)


>When I was playing yesterday, I watched my faithful dog Barkey
>step on a square that had lots of stuff on it. He ate the corpse
>that was there and then moved on. So I promptly started to scoop
>up all the stuff that was there. It turns out that my new dwavish
>mattock, the one that Barkey had just stepped on, was cursed.

>It was my impression that pets would never voluntarily step on
>something cursed. Am I wrong?

It is my impression that it only goes for the TOP item in the pile.
In addition, if food is there (like tripe), you pet will eat it
regardless of what is cursed/uncursed.  I believe you can even train
your pet to step on cursed items by throwing food on cursed items
[Why would you want this? I don't know].

>So now that I am wielding this -1 cursed Dwarvish mattock, how do
>I uncurse it? (I know that if I can find a nymph, she might steal it
>but I would like to keep it.) Dip in holy water? Read an enchant weapon
>scroll?

1. Enchant weapon scroll
2. Remove curse scroll
3. #Dip in holy water
4. #Dip in a fountain [Probability of working]
5. #Pray and hope he does the right thing.

>A couple other questions...

>How do I train my pet to steal? Do I wait until he takes something
>and then drop some food nearby him?

Wait for him/her to steal and throw tripe.

>What about this conversion of altars bit. Is a unicorn sacrifice needed?
No.  Any monster corpse (that you would normally sacrifice) can convert
an altar.  I believe bigger monsters give a better change of conversion.

>Speaking of unicorns, why do you want to give them gems (and besides,
>how exactly do you give them to him?)
Throwing gems at unicorns increasing luck.  Throwing identified gems
ant aligned unicorns increases luck alot.  Throwing identified gems at
unaligned unicorns is not recommended.

